# Klipper Firmware Setup for Mac + Raspberry Pi + FLY Super Pro 8

This guide works on a Mac.

This guide is a complete start-to-end firmware setup for a Raspberry Pi to a FLY Super Pro 8.

---

## First Raspberry Pi boot

After inserting the SD card and powering on the Raspberry Pi for the first time:

**Do not remove power during the initial boot.**

The system performs setup and filesystem expansion during the first boot. Interrupting this can corrupt the SD card.

Place the SD card into your Raspberry Pi.

Turn your VzBot on and wait for it to boot.

Once the login screen appears on your VzBot screen, open Terminal on your Mac:

```text
CMD + Spacebar → type Terminal → press Enter
```

Connect with SSH:

```bash
ssh pi@vzbot330.local
```

You can replace `vzbot330.local` with your Raspberry Pi IP address.

This guide assumes:

```text
Username: pi
Hostname: vzbot330
```

If you get a warning like:

```text
WARNING: REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED!
```

run:

```bash
ssh-keygen -R vzbot330.local
```

Then connect again:

```bash
ssh pi@vzbot330.local
```

Type `yes` when prompted, then enter your password.

Once you see:

```bash
pi@vzbot330:~ $
```

you are logged into your Raspberry Pi.

---

## Update the Raspberry Pi

Run the following commands one by one.

If prompted to confirm, type `y` and press Enter.

```bash
sudo apt-get update
```

```bash
sudo apt-get upgrade
```

---

## Build Klipper firmware for USB or CAN

Go to the Klipper folder:

```bash
cd ~/klipper/
```

`make clean` is not required for a first build. However, always run it if:

- you change microcontroller settings
- you switch communication type, for example USB to CAN
- you are reflashing after a failed attempt

It removes previous build artifacts and prevents hard-to-debug issues.

```bash
make clean
```

Open Klipper firmware configuration:

```bash
make menuconfig
```

In `menuconfig`, move with the keyboard arrows.

Enable:

```text
Extra low-level configuration
```

Use these settings for USB: 

```text
Micro-controller Architecture: STM32
Processor model: STM32H723
Clock Reference: 25 MHz
Communication interface: USB PA11/PA12
USB ids: leave as is
GPIO pins to set at micro-controller startup: !PF8
```

Use these settings for CAN: 

```text
Micro-controller Architecture: STM32
Processor model: STM32H723
Clock Reference: 25 MHz
Communication interface: CAN bus PB8/PB9
CAN bus Speed: 1000000
GPIO pins to set at micro-controller startup: !PF8
```

Important:

- Check your exact FLY Super Pro 8 processor before choosing the processor model.
- `!PF8` is specific to the FLY Super Pro 8 H723 reference setup.
- Do not change the GPIO value unless you know your board pin mapping.

Press:

```text
Q
Y
```

Build the firmware:

```bash
make
```

---

## Copy the firmware file from the Raspberry Pi to your Mac

After the build finishes, check that `klipper.bin` exists:

```bash
ls ~/klipper/out/
```

You should see:

```text
klipper.bin
```

Check the full path:

```bash
realpath ~/klipper/out/klipper.bin
```

Expected result:

```text
/home/pi/klipper/out/klipper.bin
```

Now open a new Terminal window on your Mac.

Important: do **not** SSH into the Raspberry Pi in this new Terminal window.

From the new Mac Terminal window, copy the firmware file to your Mac desktop:

```bash
scp pi@vzbot330.local:/home/pi/klipper/out/klipper.bin ~/Desktop/
```

Enter your Raspberry Pi password when asked.

Once the file is on your Mac desktop, rename it exactly to:

```text
firmware.bin
```

You will need an additional formatted MicroSD card for flashing the FLY Super Pro 8 board.

A small MicroSD card is enough, even 1 GB. Format it as FAT32.

Copy `firmware.bin` onto the formatted MicroSD card.

---

## Shut down the Raspberry Pi before powering off

From the SSH Terminal window, shut down the Raspberry Pi:

```bash
sudo shutdown -h now
```

This shuts down the Raspberry Pi safely before switching off the printer power.

Always shut down the Raspberry Pi before turning the printer power off. This avoids hard shutdowns, which can cause long-term SD card and filesystem issues.

After the Raspberry Pi has shut down, power off the printer.

---

## Flash the FLY Super Pro 8

Important:

Always power off the printer before inserting or removing the SD card from the FLY Super Pro 8 board.

Failure to do so can:

- corrupt the SD card
- interrupt flashing
- damage the board in rare cases

I recommend unplugging the Raspberry Pi power cable during the board flashing step. This makes the flash process cleaner and avoids unnecessary power state confusion.

Insert the MicroSD card with `firmware.bin` into the FLY Super Pro 8 board.

Power on the printer and wait.

The flash is probably completed within a few seconds, but wait one or two minutes to be safe.

Power off the printer again.

Remove the MicroSD card from the FLY Super Pro 8 board.

You can plug the Raspberry Pi power cable back in.

On your computer, check the content of the MicroSD card.

If the flash was successful, the file should have changed to:

```text
FLY.CUR
```

---

## Set the Raspberry Pi as a secondary MCU

SSH back into the Raspberry Pi:

```bash
ssh pi@vzbot330.local
```

Run the following commands one by one:

```bash
cd ~/klipper/
```

```bash
sudo cp ./scripts/klipper-mcu.service /etc/systemd/system/
```

```bash
sudo systemctl enable klipper-mcu.service
```

Go back to the Klipper folder:

```bash
cd ~/klipper/
```

Open Klipper firmware configuration:

```bash
make menuconfig
```

Choose:

```text
Micro-controller Architecture: Linux process
```

Make sure the GPIO field is empty.

Remove:

```text
!PF8
```

Press:

```text
Q
Y
```

Then run:

```bash
sudo service klipper stop
```

```bash
make flash
```

```bash
sudo service klipper start
```

